{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Which is shooting up for you from the field.</b> The plague of locusts was in Nisan, for in Nisan the trees begin to put forth blossoms and the buds appeared in the land; and the plague of hail that preceded it was in the month of Adar, since all ten plagues were in a single year, as they said of blessed memory: the judgment of Egypt in Egypt was twelve months. And since the hail that came in Adar broke all the trees, and the flax and the barley that had already sprouted were struck by the plague of hail, afterward, after a month's interval, in Nisan, in which Israel were redeemed, the trees sprouted and put forth blossoms, and also the wheat and the spelt, which were not struck by the plague of hail because they were late-growing, sprouted. Therefore it is written: 'which is shooting up for you from the field,' for the plague of locusts will now come to uproot every growth and all that the hail left — thus explained the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And we need</b> to say according to his words that three plagues were in Nisan, and the seven preceding ones were in eleven months; if so, the matter would come out that the plague would last more than seven days, and the days of respite between each plague and the next would be thirty days. But from the verse (Exodus 7:25), 'and seven days were fulfilled,' we learn that the respite between each plague and the next was seven days, and so all the plagues, all of them. Therefore we must equalize the durations of the plagues and the days of respite in a uniform manner, and we say that on the fifteenth of Nisan was the beginning of Moses' prophecy at the burning bush, and the Holy One, blessed be He, entreated him for seven days to go on his mission, as He proved to him from the verse 'also since yesterday, also since the day before yesterday' (Exodus 5:7), and those seven days were the seven days of Passover for the following year, as I hinted at in the verse 'from the midst of the bush' (Exodus 3:2). After the twenty-first of Nisan he went to Midian immediately, when he should have gone to Egypt, but he needed to go to Midian in order to release the oath he had sworn to Jethro, that he would not depart from the land except with his permission; and this is what is written (Exodus 4:18): 'and Moses went and returned to Jether his father-in-law,' and it is written (ibid.): 'and HaShem said to Moses in Midian' — He said to him, 'in Midian: you swore in Midian — go and release your oath in Midian.' He went to Egypt, and Moses and Aaron appeared before the king and warned him, 'Thus says HaShem,' etc., and he replied, 'I do not know HaShem,' and further said to them, 'Why, Moses and Aaron,' etc. (Exodus 5:2, 4). From the day they warned him he made the labor heavier upon them, and Moses turned and said to the Holy One, blessed be He, 'Why have You done evil' (Exodus 5:22), and He answered him, 'Now you shall see' (Exodus 6:1). All this, according to the plain sense of the verses, could have taken place within eight days, by the end of Nisan. Then they were hidden from them for three months — Iyyar, Sivan, Tammuz — as they expounded from the verse (Exodus 5:20) 'and they met' — you find that the plagues began in Av, and the plague of blood lasted seven days in Av, and the days of respite came after it, and likewise the plague of frogs in Elul, and the days of respite came, and so all of them, up to Adar, in which was the plague of locusts, and the plague of darkness in Nisan seven days, and the days of respite after it seven days — that is fourteen; and the plague of the firstborn on the night of the fifteenth of Nisan, which is the day of redemption. And with this explanation the days of the plagues' duration and the days of respite come out in equal times; and what they said, 'the judgment of Egypt was twelve months,' is not to say that the plagues stood for such a duration, but rather from the day Moses came before the king at the end of Nisan and said to him, 'Thus says HaShem: Israel is My firstborn son' (Exodus 4:22), 'behold, I will slay your son, your firstborn' (Exodus 4:23) — from that hour the judgment took hold upon them and the sentence was decreed upon them until the completion of twelve months, in which all the plagues concluded and Israel were redeemed in it."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:32.635601726Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}